<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1632–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Narai the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narai was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> from 1656 to 1688. He opened Siam to wide foreign contacts, exchanging embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and employing the Greek adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-phaulkon">Constantine Phaulkon</a>, and he made Lopburi a second royal residence. His forces destroyed the rebellious sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songkhla">Singora</a> in 1680. His death was followed by the revolution of 1688 and the expulsion of the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narai.jpg" alt="Narai" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clestur at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
